 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Occlusion Culling Granularity](1671-occlusion-culling-granularity.md)
+Configures the spatial resolution for raster occlusion culling to match the leaf-level granularity of a reference ray-marching octree. It ensures that the culling proxy geometry aligns with the underlying extraction data structures for direct performance comparison.
+
+### [Volumetric Occlusion Culling via Occlusion Queries](4594-volumetric-occlusion-culling-via-occlusion-queries.md)
+Determines if a voxel block is visible by rasterizing its bounding box and checking for fragment depth test passes. It differs from standard binary occlusion culling by incorporating accumulated opacity into the depth test, ensuring that blocks behind fully opaque foreground volumes are correctly culled.
+
+### [Hierarchical Raster Occlusion Culling](6061-hierarchical-raster-occlusion-culling.md)
+A fully GPU-driven technique that reduces rasterization overhead by performing visibility tests on a coarse hierarchy of group nodes rather than individual leaf objects, refining results via ray traversal to the leaf level. It distinguishes itself from standard Raster Occlusion Culling by replacing the exhaustive rasterization of all leaf proxies with a hierarchical culling pass, and from Iterative Raster Occlusion Culling by utilizing a hybrid raster-ray approach for full hierarchy descent.
+
 ### Hierarchical Visibility Mask
-A visibility culling technique where triangles are marked as visible and forwarded to graphics hardware rather than being rendered by the CPU, allowing the GPU to determine actual visibility and handle occlusion [S4, book]. It projects the polygon onto the top meta-tile of a hierarchical structure and recursively checks only those subtiles in which the polygon might be visible, leading to a logarithmic search [S4, book]. If a meta-cell size is approximately the size of a single pixel, recursion stops and a single point with an associated normal is forwarded to the graphics hardware, with the normal estimated by calculating the gradient of the field [S4, book]. It differs from Greene's implementation by not rendering the visible portion on the CPU; instead, it relies on the graphics accelerator to determine visible pieces and render them correctly [S4, book].
+A visibility culling technique where triangles are marked as visible and forwarded to graphics hardware rather than being rendered by the CPU, allowing the GPU to determine actual visibility and handle occlusion [S5, book]. It projects the polygon onto the top meta-tile of a hierarchical structure and recursively checks only those subtiles in which the polygon might be visible, leading to a logarithmic search [S5, book]. If a meta-cell size is approximately the size of a single pixel, it stops recursion and forwards a single point with an associated normal to the graphics hardware [S5, book]. The normal is estimated by calculating the gradient of the field [S5, book]. It differs from Greene's implementation by not rendering the visible portion on the CPU; instead, it relies on the graphics accelerator to determine visible pieces and render them correctly [S5, book].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The basic method replaces expensive individual hardware occlusion queries with one efficient proxy geometry rasterization pass [S1, S2]. Hierarchical variants achieve logarithmic search time for visibility testing due to their structure [S4, book].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the min-max texture ($X/U \times Y/V \times Z/W$ 2-channel volume texture) [S3, unofficial] and visibility buffers [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Outperforms methods that process the depth texture of a previously established occluder representation, such as hierarchical occlusion maps [ZMHH97] and iterative depth warping [LKE18] [S1, S2].
-*   **Scaling:** Better utilization of streaming multiprocessors when using compute shaders for PVS compactification compared to mesh shaders [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces the number of task shader workgroups launched, avoiding geometry extraction for invisible blocks [S2][S3, unofficial]. Fewer primitives (2 triangles per block vs 6) increase the likelihood of early-depth termination via the representative fragment test [S2][S3, unofficial]. Conservative visibility determination ensures no occupied blocks are incorrectly culled [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires precomputed min-max values for each block [S3, unofficial]. Multi-view rendering requires careful handling of proxy geometry per eye [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use it:** For volumes with high depth complexity where reducing draw times is critical [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use it:** When probabilistic culling is acceptable and hierarchical structures are already available [S3, unofficial].
+-   **Time Complexity:** The culling pass is dominated by the rasterization of proxy geometry and the subsequent stream compaction. The task shader processes blocks in groups of 256, and the mesh shader processes 32 blocks per workgroup [S4, unofficial]. The compute shader performs bit compactification, which is efficient for dense lists [S4, unofficial].
+-   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the min-max texture ($\frac{X}{U} \times \frac{Y}{V} \times \frac{Z}{W}$ 2-channel volume texture) [S4, unofficial], the visibility buffer, and the PVS lists [S3][S4, unofficial].
+-   **Measured Performance:** Outperforms methods that process depth textures of previously established occluder representations, such as hierarchical occlusion maps [ZMHH97] and iterative depth warping [LKE18] [S1, S3]. It is effective for scenes with high depth complexity where the fragment shader is heavy, as it reduces the number of task shader workgroups launched [S3][S4, unofficial].
+-   **Scaling:** Not scalable for very high polygon counts in general scenes, but effective for volume data because the fragment shader cost dominates [S2, unofficial]. The method is conservative, ensuring no occupied blocks are incorrectly culled, unlike probabilistic methods [S4, unofficial].
+-   **Strengths:** Low draw times for high depth complexity; efficient use of GPU hardware via task/mesh shaders; leverages early-depth termination via representative fragment test [S3][S4, unofficial].
+-   **Limitations:** Requires precomputed min-max textures [S4, unofficial]; conservative culling may still process some occluded blocks if the min-max bounds are loose [S3][S4, unofficial].
+-   **When to use:** When rendering dense volume data with high depth complexity and heavy fragment shaders [S2, unofficial][S3].
+-   **When not to use:** When probabilistic culling is acceptable and hierarchical structures are already available for other purposes [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Raster Occlusion Culling was introduced by Kubisch and Tavenrath [KT14] as the state-of-the-art approach for general scenes [S1, S2]. Historically, GPU-driven occlusion culling was facilitated by occlusion query objects [CCG*07], which required careful use of spatial and temporal coherence to be effective, as shown by Bittner et al. [BWPP04] and Mattausch et al. [MBW08] [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. Follow-up works include Lee and Lee [LL20] (iterative version on hierarchy levels) and Lee et al. [LJSL21] (fully GPU-driven hierarchical algorithm refining visibility via ray traversal) [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. The method described here is a hybrid between basic raster occlusion culling [KT14] and hierarchical raster occlusion culling [LL20], using a custom task and mesh shader pipeline to efficiently detect occupied blocks before creating proxy geometry [S3, unofficial]. It is distinct from probabilistic culling methods like those by Ibrahim et al. [IRR*22] [S3, unofficial].
+Raster Occlusion Culling was introduced by Kubisch and Tavenrath [KT14] as the state-of-the-art approach for efficient visibility determination for general scenes [S1, S3]. It replaced earlier GPU-driven occlusion culling methods that relied on occlusion query objects [CCG*07], which required careful exploitation of spatial and temporal coherence [S1, S3]. Follow-up works include Lee and Lee's [LL20] iterative version on hierarchy levels and Lee et al.'s [LJSL21] fully GPU-driven hierarchical algorithm refining visibility via ray traversal [S1, S3]. The method described here is a hybrid between basic raster occlusion culling [KT14] and hierarchical raster occlusion culling [LL20], resolving potential visibility only for fixed-size blocks using a custom task and mesh shader pipeline [S4, unofficial].
 
